The initialiser is an action setter too, and the trace could not see it.

CIDER_TRACE_MENUITEM_DECODE hooks -[NSMenuItem setTarget:] and -[NSMenuItem setAction:], and
-[NSMenuItem initWithTitle:action:keyEquivalent:] assigns _action directly without going through
either. Every menu item an application builds in CODE therefore read as having no action at all.

THAT SILENCE WAS RECORDED AS A DEFECT. The standing note on MoneyMoney says about eighteen items
end up with a real target and a NULL action. Measured with the trace as it was, on a settle only
drive: 108 distinct item titles, 25 of them ending with a real target and no action. Ten of the
twenty five are menu BAR items whose target is their own NSMenu and which correctly have no action;
four more are popup button items whose target is the cell. The remaining ten are real commands,
About MoneyMoney and eight Help menu entries and Encrypt File for Sending, all of them targeting
MainWindowController.

And all ten decode as action=ABSENT target=nil with ZERO control connectors, which means the nib
never gave them anything and the application builds them itself. An item built with
initWithTitle:action:keyEquivalent: has its action from birth and this trace never saw it.

So the number is an artefact of an incomplete instrument, not evidence of a defect. Adding the
initialiser to the trace is what makes the next measurement mean anything.

Instrument only, behind the same environment variable, no behaviour change.

--- a/AppKit/NSMenu.subproj/NSMenuItem.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSMenu.subproj/NSMenuItem.m
@@ -160,6 +160,14 @@
     _title = [title copy];
     _target = nil;
     _action = action;
+    /* THE INITIALISER IS AN ACTION SETTER, and leaving it out of the trace made every item an
+     * application creates in code read as having no action at all. Ten MoneyMoney commands were
+     * recorded as a defect on that silence. */
+    if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_MENUITEM_DECODE") != NULL) {
+        fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_ITEMSET init title=%s sel=%s\n",
+                [_title UTF8String] ?: "(nil)", action != NULL ? sel_getName(action) : "NULL");
+        fflush(stderr);
+    }
     _keyEquivalent = [keyEquivalent copy];
     /* COMMAND IS THE DEFAULT, as it is on Apple systems. An application that writes
      * setKeyEquivalent: @"h" means Command H and does not say so; LibreOffice sets Hide, Hide
